A||||15||Master_Sushi||Strange Injector Prob on 84SE||||1||
Z||000000||Master_Sushi||11-22-2001||05:07 PM||||This has me really confused. My 84 just wouldn't start one day. So I started working on it and found that it wasn't geting any fuel. I checked and the fuel pump IS fine. If I run 12V directly to the injector it will spray lots of gas in a nice even pattern. After the car is basicly primed by hotwireing the injector I can turn it over and it will run just fine. But if I turn it off and try to start again I dont get any fuel.<P>Basicly my car will ONLY start on a prime, and will run fine after it starts. But it wont let a drop of gas go unless I prime it first, then it will be fine.<P>I have no idea whats wrong.<P><BR>Help Please!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>Oh, and if all else fails im thinking of runing a switch to jump 12V to the injector. Sort of a Prime button or something. I'd rather not do this if I don't have to though!<p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 11-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||reddog||11-22-2001||08:50 PM||||What kind of engine do you have? What Tranny?Can you hear the fuel pump when you turn the ignition to the on position.Could be the fuel pump relay!!How is your oil pressure? Check your cold start switch!<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by reddog (edited 11-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Master_Sushi||11-22-2001||09:12 PM||||I have the only engine offered in 84 fieros  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">, with a 4 speed stick. Yes I hear the fuel pump and its fine. Oil pressure, im not sure I dont have the gauge for it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">, haven't had any idiot lights recently though...<P>Where is the cold start switch located and whats it do?||||reg||1
Z||000003||reddog||11-22-2001||09:31 PM||||On the 2.8 the cold start switch is located on the crank pulley side(battery side)and is located next to the cooling fan switch. I'm not sure if the 2.5 even has one. Anyway it is a switch that activates the cold start injector which injects extra fuel into the intake when starting the engine during cooler weather. But if you have the 4 banger then I don't think I can help you. The reason that I ask about the oil pressure was that I read on the forum somewhere that the fuel pump won't operate if there is no oil pressure. The two are connected to the same relay or something like that.I hope you find problem I'm sure another forum member can help you!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||johnt671||11-23-2001||08:08 AM||||I had the same problem with my 84.  If your fuel pump is bad the fuel pump will not pump until the oil pressure light goes out as you are cranking the engine.  That didn't help mine so I gave up and put it in the shop.  Turned out to be a bad computer.  Try the fuel pump relay 1st. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Master_Sushi||11-23-2001||09:30 AM||||Ya, I know for sure its not the relay because I even put in a bypass switch on it. I am starting to think its the ECM also. If I don't figure it out, today i'm going out and geting a button to wire up on it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||maryjane||11-23-2001||09:40 AM||||I have an 84 4 cyl. I don't think there is a cold start valve on this eng. First make sure both the fuel pump relay and the oil press switch are good. Then check for correct fuel press at the filter line, BUT, this sounds like an electronic problem, the ecm, mayby bad wiring to the fuel injector itself. Without the power jumped to the injector, what happens if you were to pour a little gas down the tbi? ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Master_Sushi||11-23-2001||09:43 AM||||If I just pour a little gas down the TBI.... THe engine will crank and then start. Then once its started the TBI will start firing gas and it will drive perfectly  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">.<P>THis is why its so strange, once the car is started it will drive fine, but to start it I have to manualy put gas into it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Master_Sushi||11-23-2001||05:28 PM||||bump||||reg||1
Z||000009||maryjane||11-23-2001||06:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Master_Sushi:<BR><B>If I just pour a little gas down the TBI.... THe engine will crank and then start. Then once its started the TBI will start firing gas and it will drive perfectly   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> .<P>THis is why its so strange, once the car is started it will drive fine, but to start it I have to manualy put gas into it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Well, welcome to the Fiero club. This problem surfaces here periodically, seems no one has really found a fix. That's why I asked what would happen if you poured gas down the TBI. I at first thought vapor lock,relocated the fuel line and filter away from the eng and insulated it. It worked for a while. It only happened after driving the car for a while. Never died while running, just wouldn't fire off after I shut it off. Wouldn't start till it cooled off about 20 minutes. Changed the fuel pump relay, it took care of it except on the hottest days. I suspect a failing ecm, but not sure. <BR>I wish Ogre would address this problem and the question of whether the oil presure switch will kill the eng by design if the oil press drops below 4psi. (assuming the oil press switch and the fuel press relay are functioning properly.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Master_Sushi||11-23-2001||07:16 PM||||Well im off to Radio Shack. Im going to get a button set up that runs 12 volts to the injector when pressed to prime the engine for starting. I allready have a switch for my fuel pump, headlights, fan, and amp, and soon a button for the injector   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> . Starting my car is going to be like geting an airplace ready for takeoff. lol<P>Maybe ill start a post and see if the orge know anything about this.<P>Thanks all<p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 11-23-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000011||theogre||11-23-2001||11:52 PM||||Could be a number of things. Did anyone bother to read the cheap Haynes book?<P>Chapter 4.... on Page 132, section 3 Fuel pump testing.....<P>item 4. An Inoperative Fuel Pump Relay can result in long crank time, especially if the engine is cold.<P>The relay doens't have to be totaly dead. If the points are burnt then it may not pass full curent to the pump... It will run but not get full pressure.<P>If the relay has been replaced you need to see if the ECM is providing 12V to terminal A of the relay connector. (Drak Green/White wire.) The ECM should power up that line as soon as you turn the key to run. If you don't start the car it will shut off after a few seconds so you'll likely need 2 people for this test. IF the ECM is powering that line then the ECM is likely ok. After that it's a wire problem with one or more relay leads.<P>starting and staying running after dumping fuel in the TBI tells you the fuel pump and fuel pressure regulator are working. Or at least they appear to. We'll get to that more in a minute.<P>You might also want to replace the oil pressure switch. If it's also on the edge of dead then crank time can be longer still.<P>The fuel pump running doesn't always mean it's good. you need to check fuel pressure as well. gently pinch a soft section the fuel return line to block it's flow. Pinch with pliers but wrap the jaws with tape to protect the hose from sharp edges. If the car starts then either the fuel pump is bad or the fuel pressure regulator is.<P>BTW... hard wireing a 12V feed to the injector... bad idea. You'll likely cook the injector if you keep doing that. The injector does run on 12V but the current is limited in the ECM driver circuit.<P>If you really think there's an injector problem buy a test light (called a noid light) for about $5-10 from an autoparts store. plug it into the harness and crank the car. If the ECM is sending power the light will light/flash.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">     <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||maryjane||11-24-2001||12:36 AM||||I've done the pressure test, got about 12 psi, it increased to 14 when I pinched the  return line. New fuel pump relay and new oil pressure switch. It rarely gives me trouble now that the weather has turned cool, so it's managable for me. Going to try an ecm change next, never been changed since the car was new.<BR>quote "You might also want to replace the oil pressure switch. If it's on the edge of dead then crank time can be longer still"<BR>I still don't understand what the significance of the oil press switch is in relation to a good operating fuel press relay. If the fuel press relay is working properly, the oil press switch plays no part in starting the car. The circuit is closed all the way to the fuel pump, anytime the fuel press relay is closed, and the relay is getting it's signal from the ecm as long as the dist is giving out ign pulses. I've disconnected my oil press sw & the eng will start perfectly. It is only there to back-up the fuel press relay, & send info to the idiot light on the dash, isn't it? ||||reg||1
Z||000013||johnt671||11-24-2001||10:17 AM||||The back up to a failed fuel pump relay is that the fuel pump will kick on in a bypass mode if you crank the car long enough to build up the oil pressure high enough to make the oil pressure light go off.  A weak oil pressure switch takes longer to  read the oil pressure therefore longer to let the car start.  Haynes manual explains this in more detail.||||reg||1
Z||000014||maryjane||11-24-2001||10:38 AM||||But that is ONLY in the event of a failed FP relay. If the relay is working, the OP switch plays no part in normal starting of eng. That's why I said, you can disconnect the op sw, & car will still start. Disconnect it with eng running, car doesn't die. "IF", the FP relay is operating properly. I've tried it. It's that way.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Master_Sushi||11-24-2001||12:48 PM||||I know my fuel pump is good also. The car runs just fine when its started. Im going to put the switch in today because I need my car on monday. I measured 30 ohms at the TBI with my meter. So Ill just put a 30 ohm resistor on with my ground runing to the injector. If it still toasts the injector..... oh well||||reg||1
